disk: refuse to format a device Anunix did not write

Implements RFC-0031 section 8, which this commit also adds to the RFC.

main.c formatted the active block device at boot whenever it failed to find
an object store on it:

    if (ds_ret != ANX_OK) {
            /* First boot on this disk - format automatically */
            ds_ret = anx_disk_format("anunix");

anx_disk_format() read nothing before writing. No partition table check, no
filesystem check, no prompt. Combined with "the first device registered
becomes the active device", booting Anunix on a machine where any drive
bound reformatted that drive.

This is not theoretical. Booting the 2026.9.4 ISO under UEFI against a disk
partitioned like jekyll left sector 0 reading 44 58 4e 41 -- "DXNA", the
little-endian ANX_DISK_MAGIC -- where the protective MBR had been. sgdisk
reported "invalid main GPT header, but valid backup". On jekyll's nvme0n1
that would have been the Fedora installation.

The only thing that has prevented this costing a machine is an unrelated
defect: the anxboot EFI stub identity-maps 4 GiB, UEFI firmware puts the
NVMe BAR above it, and the driver page-faults during probe. No block device
registers, so nothing is formatted. Fixing that mapping without this commit
would arm the failure rather than remove it.

  - anx_blk_probe() classifies a device as BLANK, ANUNIX or FOREIGN by
    reading sectors 0, 1, 2, 8 and 128. It recognises GPT, MBR, Linux RAID
    1.x, Anunix RAID, LUKS, XFS, ext2/3/4, btrfs, FAT and NTFS, and reports
    a short description so a refusal can say why.
  - Anything unreadable is FOREIGN. An unreadable disk is not an empty one,
    and guessing in the permissive direction costs data.
  - Non-zero bytes in no format we recognise are FOREIGN too. An unknown
    layout is still someone's.
  - anx_disk_format() now probes first and refuses FOREIGN (ANX_EEXIST) and
    array members (ANX_EBUSY). anx_disk_format_forced() is the explicit
    path, used by the installer after its confirmation prompt.
  - main.c no longer formats. Absent a store it logs which device it looked
    at and what is on it, then runs from memory. Running without
    persistence is recoverable; formatting someone's disk is not.

The shell's "store format" keeps the guard deliberately, so an operator who
types it at a partitioned disk is told what is on it rather than losing it.

tests/test_blk_probe.c asserts the refusals, not just the successes: a
refused format is checked to have left sector 0 byte-identical. Every probe
signature has a case. 62 passed, 0 failed.

Verified end to end under QEMU/UEFI against a GPT disk laid out like
jekyll, with NVMe bound so the old path would have fired:

    part: nvme0p2 on nvme0 lba 206848+524288 ANUNIX_RAID0_A
    disk: no object store on nvme0 (holds MBR partition table)
    disk: running without persistence; install to create a store

The image was byte-identical afterwards and sgdisk reported no problems.

